      "sections": [
        {
          "section_type": "example_question",
          "normalized_html": "<p>Rewrite the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: (x^2 - 12x + 36) -5]</span> as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.</p>",
          "readable_text": "Rewrite the expression [MATH: (x^2 - 12x + 36) -5] as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.",
          "images": [],
          "free_entry_blanks": [],
          "select_lists": []
        },
        {
          "section_type": "content",
          "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
          "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
          "images": [],
          "free_entry_blanks": [],
          "select_lists": []
        },
        {
          "section_type": "example_explanation",
          "normalized_html": "<p>First, we recall the following formula:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2]</span></p><p>Notice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: x^{2} \u2212 12x + 36|=; x^{2} \u2212 12x + 6^{2}|=; x^{2} \u2212 2 \u22c5 6x + 6^{2}|=; (x \u2212 6)^{2}]</span></p><p>Therefore, we obtain\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: (x^{2} \u2212 12x + 36) \u2212 5|=; (x \u2212 6)^{2} \u2212 5|.]</span></p>",
          "readable_text": "First, we recall the following formula:\n[MATH: x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2]\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n[MATH: x^{2} \u2212 12x + 36|=; x^{2} \u2212 12x + 6^{2}|=; x^{2} \u2212 2 \u22c5 6x + 6^{2}|=; (x \u2212 6)^{2}]\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n[MATH: (x^{2} \u2212 12x + 36) \u2212 5|=; (x \u2212 6)^{2} \u2212 5|.]",
          "images": [],
          "free_entry_blanks": [],
          "select_lists": []
        }
      ],
      "images": [],
      "free_entry_blanks": [],
      "select_lists": [],
      "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nRewrite the expression [MATH: (x^2 - 12x + 36) -5] as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we recall the following formula:\n[MATH: x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2]\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n[MATH: x^{2} \u2212 12x + 36|=; x^{2} \u2212 12x + 6^{2}|=; x^{2} \u2212 2 \u22c5 6x + 6^{2}|=; (x \u2212 6)^{2}]\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n[MATH: (x^{2} \u2212 12x + 36) \u2212 5|=; (x \u2212 6)^{2} \u2212 5|.]"
